# Working Memory. S = questions in slide order, R = connection questions.
S = [
 ([2], "Why is treating short-term memory as passive storage too simple?", "Keeping information available also involves control processes, which are operations that act on that information. A memory model has to account for both holding something in mind and doing something with it."),
 ([2], "What is working memory according to Baddeley and Hitch (1974)?", "Working memory is a limited-capacity system for the temporary storage and manipulation of information. Storage keeps information available for immediate use, and manipulation changes or works with it while it remains available."),
 ([2], "Why do comprehension, learning and reasoning need working memory?", "They can require storage and manipulation together. Comprehension keeps information available while relating its parts, and reasoning maintains the information used in a decision while performing the operations that lead to an answer."),
 ([2], "What does manipulation mean in Baddeley and Hitch’s (1974) definition of working memory?", "Manipulation changes or works with information while it remains available, as opposed to storage, which keeps information available for immediate use."),
 ([3], "How does mentally multiplying 43 times 6 use both storage and processing?", "First 3 × 6 = 18. The 8 is held as the ones digit, which is storage, and the 1 is carried over the 4, which is an operation. Then 6 × 4 = 24, plus the carried 1 gives 25, and combining it with the held 8 gives 258."),
 ([3], "What does working memory account for in the 43 × 6 calculation?", "It accounts for keeping an intermediate result available while changing other parts of the problem, and for coordinating which number is stored, which is carried and which operation is performed."),
 ([4], "How does Atkinson and Shiffrin’s (1968) short-term memory model differ from Baddeley and Hitch’s (1974) working-memory model?", "In Atkinson and Shiffrin’s model a single component holds different types of information for a short time. Baddeley and Hitch’s model separates the system into the central executive, the visuospatial sketchpad and the phonological loop."),
 ([4], "What is the episodic buffer in relation to the original working-memory model?", "It is a fourth component added by Baddeley in 2000. It belongs to the expanded model and not to the original three-component proposal."),
 ([5], "What does the phonological loop do, and what are its two parts?", "It maintains verbal and auditory information. Its phonological store holds that information, and its articulatory rehearsal process keeps it active through rehearsal."),
 ([5], "What does articulatory mean, and how does articulatory rehearsal work?", "Articulatory refers to producing speech. Rehearsal can involve repeating the sounds internally rather than aloud, which refreshes information that would otherwise decay in the store."),
 ([5], "What does the visuospatial sketchpad hold?", "Visual and spatial information. Visual information concerns the appearance of something, and spatial information concerns where its parts are located and how they are arranged."),
 ([5], "What does the central executive do?", "It pulls information from long-term memory, coordinates the other components, and directs and maintains attention. It controls how the temporary stores contribute to the task and is not a store for a particular kind of information."),
 ([6], "How do the working-memory components interact with each other and with long-term knowledge?", "They form interacting parts rather than a single store, so a task can use more than one kind of representation, such as verbal rehearsal of a result with a visual picture of the digits. Long-term knowledge is connected to the components and can be made available during a task."),
 ([6], "How can a calculation use both verbal and visual representations?", "The connections between components allow a task to use more than one kind of representation, such as verbal rehearsal of a result together with a visual representation of the digits."),
 ([7], "What is the phonological similarity effect?", "It occurs when letters or words that sound similar are confused. Items are hard to keep distinct when their sound patterns overlap, even if their written forms differ."),
 ([7], "What is the word-length effect?", "Memory for lists is better for short words than for long words, because longer words take more time to rehearse and to produce during recall."),
 ([7], "What is articulatory suppression?", "It involves speaking while trying to remember other material. Speaking occupies the rehearsal process, so it interferes with the repetition that keeps verbal items available."),
 ([7], "How do the three phonological loop effects concern different aspects of verbal maintenance?", "Similarity concerns confusion between sound patterns, word length concerns the time required to repeat items, and suppression concerns interference with the rehearsal process itself."),
 ([8], "What did Conrad (1964) do?", "Conrad (1964) briefly flashed target letters, similar in half of the presentations and dissimilar in half, and asked participants to write them down."),
 ([8], "What kind of errors did Conrad’s (1964) participants make?", "They more often substituted a letter that sounded like the target than one that looked like it. An E was likely to be replaced by a B, while a visually similar substitution such as F for E was less likely."),
 ([9], "What does Conrad’s (1964) result show about how seen letters are remembered?", "Visually presented letters can be recoded into a sound-based representation through the phonological loop, so the confusions follow the pronunciation and not the appearance."),
 ([9], "What is the difference between presentation format and memory code?", "Presentation format is what participants see, while the memory code is the form in which the information is maintained. Letters seen visually can be maintained as sounds."),
 ([10], "What did Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984) find about word length?", "People remembered more short words than long words, because the word-length effect concerns how long items take to pronounce and rehearse."),
 ([10], "What did Ellis and Hennelly (1980) find?", "Welsh–English bilingual children had a shorter digit span in Welsh than in English, because Welsh number names take longer to pronounce, even though the children knew both languages."),
 ([10], "How is memory span related to speaking time?", "Span is related to the amount of verbal material that can be said in about 1.5 to 2 seconds, so faster rehearsal lets more items be refreshed."),
 ([11], "What does articulatory suppression do to memory?", "The competing speech keeps the phonological loop busy, so the person has less opportunity to rehearse and recall becomes worse."),
 ([11], "What happens to the word-length effect under articulatory suppression?", "It is reduced. Short words normally win because they can be rehearsed more quickly, and when rehearsal is occupied neither list gets ordinary rehearsal."),
 ([11], "What happens to the phonological similarity effect under articulatory suppression?", "It is also reduced, because verbal rehearsal and the conversion of seen items into sounds are disrupted, so sound overlap has less influence."),
 ([12], "What is the difference between visual and spatial information in the sketchpad?", "Visual information includes the appearance and shape of something, and spatial information includes the arrangement and relative locations of its parts, which allows that arrangement to remain available without continuous visual input."),
 ([12], "What does the visuospatial sketchpad support?", "Mental imagery, meaning keeping a visual representation in mind so it can be used during a task when it is no longer being viewed. Holding a shape in mind is a different demand from holding the sounds of a sentence."),
 ([13], "What was the sentence task in Brooks (1968)?", "Participants memorized a sentence and decided for each word whether it was a noun, responding either by speaking or by pointing."),
 ([13], "What did Brooks (1968) find in the sentence task, and why?", "Pointing was easier than speaking, because maintaining the sentence occupied the phonological loop and speaking the answers required verbal processing that competed with it."),
 ([14], "What was the F task in Brooks (1968)?", "Participants memorized the shape of an F, mentally followed its outline and classified each corner as an inside or an outside corner, responding by speaking or by pointing to the response locations."),
 ([14], "What did Brooks (1968) find in the F task, and why?", "Speaking was easier than pointing, because holding the F used the visuospatial sketchpad and pointing to spatially arranged responses added a competing spatial demand."),
 ([15], "What does the opposite pattern in Brooks (1968) show?", "A task is easier when the information held and the operation performed involve different types of short-term memory. The opposite response advantages support some independence between verbal and visual–spatial memory."),
 ([17], "What did Shepard and Metzler (1971) have participants do?", "They presented pairs of block-built objects at different orientations, and participants judged whether the objects matched despite the difference in orientation."),
 ([17], "What is mental rotation, and what are picture-plane and depth rotation?", "Mental rotation is turning a representation in mind to compare how its parts line up. Picture-plane rotation changes orientation within the flat viewing plane, and depth rotation changes it in three-dimensional space."),
 ([17], "What did Shepard and Metzler (1971) find about reaction time?", "For picture-plane pairs, reaction time increased as the angle of rotation increased, approximately linearly. A larger angle requires a larger mental transformation, which fits an active operation on a spatial representation."),
 ([18], "What does the central executive do in executive control?", "It directs and maintains attention, including focusing, dividing attention and switching it. It also suppresses irrelevant information, coordinates the sketchpad and the phonological loop, and performs calculations."),
 ([18], "What are updating, shifting and inhibition in Miyake and colleagues (2000)?", "Updating changes the information currently maintained, shifting changes the task or operation being performed, and inhibition suppresses information or responses that interfere with the current goal."),
 ([18], "Why is the central executive sometimes called a homunculus?", "A homunculus is a little internal agent given the job of controlling everything else. Naming a controller does not explain how control is done, so separating updating, shifting and inhibition specifies different operations."),
 ([18], "What do focusing, dividing and switching attention refer to in executive control?", "They are operations that control processing: focusing on relevant information, dividing attention across demands and switching attention when the task changes."),
 ([19], "What is the episodic buffer, and what is its capacity?", "Baddeley (2000) added it as a fourth component. It has a limited capacity of about four chunks, where a chunk is a unit that combines related information."),
 ([19], "What does the episodic buffer do?", "It binds information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes. Binding means keeping different kinds of information together as a connected representation."),
 ([19], "What evidence supports the episodic buffer?", "People can immediately recall about 16 words from a meaningful sentence, beyond their span for unrelated words, and some amnesic patients can immediately recall a just-heard story."),
 ([20], "What did Vogel and colleagues (2005) measure first?", "They measured participants’ working-memory capacity to distinguish higher- and lower-capacity groups, and then recorded event-related potentials, ERPs, which are electrical brain responses measured in relation to a task event."),
 ([20], "What was the task in Vogel and colleagues (2005)?", "A cue directed attention to one side, red bars were shown for 100 milliseconds, a 900 millisecond retention interval followed with the bars absent, and then a test display asked whether a relevant orientation had changed."),
 ([20], "Why were irrelevant blue bars included in Vogel and colleagues (2005)?", "Ignoring the blue bars required selection within the display, so comparing target-only and distractor conditions tested how irrelevant items entered working-memory processing."),
 ([21], "How did the ERP response behave in Vogel and colleagues (2005)?", "It continues into the retention interval after the display disappears. It has a negative polarity, so a larger response means a stronger negative deflection."),
 ([21], "What happened for high-capacity participants in Vogel and colleagues (2005)?", "The response was greater with four relevant items than with two, and adding two irrelevant blue items to two relevant items changed little, so they kept the distractors out."),
 ([21], "What happened for low-capacity participants in Vogel and colleagues (2005)?", "Adding the irrelevant items increased the response toward the four-item condition, so more of the irrelevant material entered working-memory processing."),
 ([22], "How are working-memory capacity and filtering efficiency related in Vogel and colleagues (2005)?", "They are positively correlated. Participants with higher capacity tend to have higher filtering efficiency, meaning they exclude irrelevant items more effectively."),
 ([22], "What does Vogel and colleagues’ (2005) result mean for individual differences in working memory?", "People with higher capacity are less distracted by irrelevant items, and a lower score can reflect competing material occupying processing that targets needed. Differences include selection and control, not only how much relevant information is retained."),
]
R = [
 ("How is working memory different from the single short-term store of the modal model?", "The modal model has one short-term component for temporary availability, while working memory has separate components for verbal, visual–spatial and integrated information, plus executive control, and it includes manipulation of information.", [2,4]),
 ("Why does the 43 × 6 example need more than a store?", "The retained 8 is storage, but carrying the 1 and performing the next multiplication and addition are operations, so the system must both hold and manipulate information.", [2,3]),
 ("How do the phonological loop and the visuospatial sketchpad differ, and how does the central executive relate to them?", "The loop maintains verbal and auditory information and the sketchpad maintains visual and spatial information. The central executive is not a store but coordinates both, directs attention and draws on long-term memory.", [5,6]),
 ("How do the three phonological loop effects relate to the same underlying process?", "Similarity depends on sound-based codes, word length depends on rehearsal time, and suppression occupies the rehearsal process, so they are different aspects of verbal maintenance, and suppression, which occupies rehearsal, also reduces the word-length and similarity effects.", [7,10,11]),
 ("Why do Conrad’s (1964) results support a sound-based code even for visual letters?", "The letters were seen, but confusions followed sound, such as E replaced by B, so the maintained code was phonological and not visual.", [8,9]),
 ("How do the word-length finding of Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984) and Ellis and Hennelly (1980) show the same principle?", "Both show that span depends on how long items take to say. Long words and Welsh number names take longer to rehearse, so fewer items fit in about 1.5 to 2 seconds.", [10]),
 ("How do the phonological store, the sketchpad and the episodic buffer each hold a different kind of information?", "The phonological store holds verbal and auditory information, the visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information, and the episodic buffer binds information from the loop, the sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes.", [5,19]),
 ("How do Brooks’s (1968) two tasks together show that verbal and visual–spatial memory are partly independent?", "The sentence task was easier with pointing and the F task was easier with speaking. In each case the response was easier when it used a different type of memory from the one holding the information.", [13,14,15]),
 ("How do Vogel and colleagues (2005) and Brooks (1968) each use a competing or irrelevant demand to learn about working memory?", "Brooks (1968) varied whether the response used speaking or pointing and found it was easier when it used a different type of memory from the one holding the information. Vogel and colleagues (2005) added irrelevant blue bars and found that higher-capacity participants kept them out.", [13,14,15,20,21]),
 ("How is the mental rotation result of Shepard and Metzler (1971) related to the idea of operating on a representation?", "In Shepard and Metzler (1971), the linear increase in reaction time with angle suggests an active transformation of a spatial representation, which matches working memory as manipulation and not only storage.", [2,17]),
 ("How does the central executive’s control differ from the contents held in the stores?", "The executive directs attention, suppresses irrelevant information and coordinates components, while the stores hold verbal or visual–spatial contents. Updating, shifting and inhibition describe operations of control.", [5,18]),
 ("Why was the central executive called a homunculus, and how do Miyake and colleagues (2000) respond?", "Naming a controller does not itself explain how control is performed, and separating updating, shifting and inhibition in Miyake and colleagues (2000) specifies different operations.", [18]),
 ("Why was the episodic buffer needed beyond the loop and the sketchpad?", "People recall about 16 words of a meaningful sentence, beyond their span for unrelated words, and amnesic patients can immediately recall a story, which suggests an integrated temporary store using long-term knowledge.", [4,19]),
 ("How do the episodic buffer’s four-chunk capacity and the 16-word sentence result fit together?", "The slide frames capacity as about four chunks, which are organized units and not necessarily four individual words, and meaningful material can exceed the span for unrelated words.", [19]),
 ("How does the filtering finding of Vogel and colleagues (2005) relate to the central executive’s functions?", "Differences in capacity include selection and control, and the executive’s functions include suppressing irrelevant information and directing attention.", [18,21,22]),
 ("Why is capacity in Vogel and colleagues (2005) about which items get in and not just how many are shown?", "High-capacity participants kept blue distractors out, while low-capacity participants let them in, so effective capacity depends on selection.", [20,21,22]),
 ("How do the tasks of Conrad (1964) and Brooks (1968) show different components of working memory?", "Conrad’s letter confusions show phonological loop coding, while Brooks’s tasks show competition within a component, speaking competing with the loop in the sentence task and pointing competing with the sketchpad in the F task.", [8,13,14]),
 ("How do Shepard and Metzler (1971) and Brooks (1968) each show what the visuospatial sketchpad does?", "Shepard and Metzler (1971) found that reaction time rose with the angle of rotation, which fits an operation on a spatial representation. Brooks (1968) found that holding the shape of an F used the sketchpad, so pointing to spatially arranged responses competed with it.", [12,14,17]),
 ("What does working memory add to a single short-term store?", "A single short-term store keeps information temporarily available, and working memory adds operations performed on held information, as in calculation, reasoning and comprehension, with separate components and executive control.", [2,3,4]),
 ("How do Conrad (1964) and Ellis and Hennelly (1980) each show that verbal memory depends on speech sounds?", "Conrad (1964) found that seen letters were confused by their spoken names, and Ellis and Hennelly (1980) found that digit span was shorter in Welsh because the number names take longer to pronounce.", [8,9,10]),
]
